const html = () => "<blockquote>\n<p>Lesson 2 of the <em>Fund Strategy</em> knowledge supplement. Maps to the email’s ”🔲 ETF Grid” and “ETF DCA Matrix” sections — first understand the ETF itself.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you learn only one instrument, learn the <strong>ETF (exchange-traded fund)</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-index-fund-vs-etf\">1. Index fund vs ETF</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Index fund</strong>: passively tracks an index (e.g. CSI 300) — “buy a basket in one click”, diversifying single-stock blow-up risk.</li>\n<li><strong>ETF</strong>: the exchange-traded version of an index fund — traded in your stock account in real time, <strong>lower fees, more transparent, live pricing</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In short: an ETF is the upgraded index fund, friendliest to retail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-four-etf-families\">2. Four ETF families</h2>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Trait</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Broad</td><td>CSI300 ETF (510300), CSI A500 ETF (563360)</td><td>Whole-market, moderate vol, DCA first choice</td></tr><tr><td>Sector</td><td>Semi / Pharma / Military ETF</td><td>Bet a theme, higher vol, satellite sleeve</td></tr><tr><td>Commodity</td><td>Gold ETF (518880)</td><td>Tracks gold, hedge + inflation</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-border</td><td>Nasdaq100 ETF (159501), S&#x26;P500 ETF (159655)</td><td>Overseas, watch premium &#x26; quota</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-to-pick-a-good-etf\">3. How to pick a good ETF?</h2>\n<p>Four hard metrics, by priority:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fees</strong>: management + custody, broad ETFs often &#x3C; 0.2%/yr — lower compounds in your favor;</li>\n<li><strong>Tracking error</strong>: smaller is better;</li>\n<li><strong>Scale &#x26; liquidity</strong>: bigger scale, higher daily turnover → tighter spreads, easier to exit;</li>\n<li><strong>History</strong>: 5+ years listed, consistently making new highs → steadier.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-best-for-ordinary-people\">4. Why best for ordinary people?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>No need to study individual financials;</li>\n<li>Low fees, far cheaper than active funds long-term;</li>\n<li>Up over time (the economy grows) but volatile mid-way — exactly where <strong>DCA + grid</strong> turn volatility into an edge.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-two-must-watch-risks\">5. Two must-watch risks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Premium / discount</strong>: common in cross-border ETFs; price may exceed (premium) or fall below (discount) NAV. Check <strong>IOPV</strong> before buying — skip if premium > 3%;</li>\n<li><strong>Quota / FX</strong>: QDII types have FX quotas; when exhausted, subscriptions pause and premium spikes.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Remember: an ETF is a <em>tool</em> — profits come from <strong>disciplined use</strong> (DCA base, grid enhancement, high-dividend ballast), not from predicting its direction.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ This article is a framework illustration and not investment or trading advice. Markets are risky; decide with care.</p>\n</blockquote>";

